    "steps": [
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 259,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 260,
        "char_start": 0,
        "effective_step": 1,
        "end_token_index": 84,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 85,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Given that \\( |a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1 \\), we know that \\( a, b, c, d \\) lie on the unit circle in the complex plane. Also, since \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\), the sum of these points is zero, which implies they form a quadrilateral centered at the origin."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 402,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 404,
        "char_start": 262,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 134,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 135,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Consider the polynomial whose roots are \\( a, b, c, d \\). This polynomial can be written as \\( P(z) = (z - a)(z - b)(z - c)(z - d) \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 628,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 629,
        "char_start": 406,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 214,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 215,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Since \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\), the polynomial can be expressed as \\( P(z) = z^4 + pz^2 + q \\) for some constants \\( p \\) and \\( q \\). The condition \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\) means the coefficient of \\( z^3 \\) is zero."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 872,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 873,
        "char_start": 631,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 290,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 291,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: To find the maximum value of \\( |(a+b)(a+c)(a+d)(b+c)(b+d)(c+d)| \\), we use the fact that if \\( a, b, c, d \\) are the vertices of a square inscribed in the unit circle, then the product of the sums of opposite sides will be maximized."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1099,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1100,
        "char_start": 875,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 393,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 394,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: If \\( a, b, c, d \\) are the vertices of a square, we can assume without loss of generality that \\( a = 1, b = i, c = -1, d = -i \\). Then,\n\\[\n(a+b)(a+c)(a+d)(b+c)(b+d)(c+d) = (1+i)(1-1)(1-i)(i-1)(i+(-i))((-1)+(-i)).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1204,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1205,
        "char_start": 1101,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 452,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 453,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Simplify the terms:\n\\[\n(1+i)(1-1)(1-i)(i-1)(i+(-i))((-1)+(-i)) = (1+i)(0)(1-i)(i-1)(0)(-1-i).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1562,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1563,
        "char_start": 1206,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 574,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 575,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Notice that the product includes several zeros, making the entire product zero. However, this configuration does not maximize the product. Instead, consider the configuration where \\( a, b, c, d \\) are the fourth roots of unity, i.e., \\( a = 1, b = -1, c = i, d = -i \\). Then,\n\\[\n(a+b)(a+c)(a+d)(b+c)(b+d)(c+d) = (1-1)(1+i)(1-i)(-1+i)(-1-i)(i-i).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1667,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1668,
        "char_start": 1564,
        "effective_step": 8,
        "end_token_index": 630,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 631,
        "step_number": 8,
        "text": "Step 8: Simplify the terms again:\n\\[\n(1-1)(1+i)(1-i)(-1+i)(-1-i)(i-i) = (0)(1+i)(1-i)(-1+i)(-1-i)(0).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1907,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1909,
        "char_start": 1669,
        "effective_step": 9,
        "end_token_index": 687,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 688,
        "step_number": 9,
        "text": "Step 9: Again, the product includes several zeros, but we need to consider the non-zero part. For the correct configuration, we can use the fact that the product of the sums of the sides of a square inscribed in the unit circle is \\( 16 \\)."
      }
